            <h2 style="font-size: 18pt;">Ed Hopkins' Historical Corner</h2>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">By Edward J. Hopkins, PhD, Assistant Wisconsin State Climatologist</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">According to NOAA's National Centers for Environmental Information (NCEI), the three warmest summers of 1936 to 1938 at the contiguous United States since records began in 1890. Although the 1936 summer was not as warm as the record summer of 1988, that summer has 85 ago long has been remembered for a killer July heat wave that ran for at least 11 days at essentially all 89 observing stations across the contiguous United States. The heat wave's worst days in 1936 were {{ temperature_high_1936 }} degrees Fahrenheit. The heat wave's worst days in 1937 were {{ temperature_high_1937 }} degrees Fahrenheit. The heat wave's worst days in 1938 were {{ temperature_high_1938 }} degrees Fahrenheit.</p>
            <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-around; margin-top: 30px;">
                <div style="text-align: center;">
                    <img src="placeholder_temp_chart.png" alt="Temperature Chart" style="max-width: 150px; height: auto;">
                    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">1936 Temperature</p>
                </div>
                <div style="text-align: center;">
                    <img src="placeholder_temp_chart_2.png" alt="Temperature Chart 2" style="max-width: 150px; height: auto;">
                    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">1937 Temperature</p>
                </div>
                <div style="text-align: center;">
                    <img src="placeholder_temp_chart_3.png" alt="Temperature Chart 3" style="max-width: 150px; height: auto;">
                    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">1938 Temperature</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt; margin-top: 20px;">A minimum temperature of {{ min_temp_1936 }} degrees Fahrenheit on the 8th, while Madison had temperatures of {{ madison_temp_1936 }} on the 11th and 13th. Unoubtedly, these elevated nighttime temperatures contributed to many deaths.</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">The 1936 heat wave was heat of record in the state. The 1938 heat wave was not as severe as the 1936 heat wave, but it was still a significant event. The 1938 heat wave was also associated with a drought condition. A nearly stationary high pressure ridge was centered over the plains in early to mid-July 1936. While Wisconsin had experienced drought in 1934, the 1936 drought was more severe, as it shifted westward across the plains during 1936, resulting in the infamous Dust Bowl. The month</p>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">of July 1936 was the driest July in Wisconsin's history, with a statewide average precipitation of {{ precip_avg_1936 }} inches, which was 2.80 inches below the July 20th century (1901-2000) average. The state's average precipitation for the driest July on record. The lack of precipitation, along with significant summer rain, along with high temperatures, led to the Dust Bowl. According to NCEI, the Palmer Drought Severity Index (PDSI) was at its most severe in {{ year_pdsi }} in the state. The lack of evaporative cooling from the lack of precipitation, along with high temperatures, led to the Dust Bowl. The Dust Bowl was a period of severe drought in the Great Plains, and Wisconsin remained in severe drought.</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">The lack of evaporative cooling from the lack of precipitation, along with high temperatures, led to the Dust Bowl. The Dust Bowl was a period of severe drought in the Great Plains, and Wisconsin remained in severe drought.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

