            <h2 style="font-size: 18pt;">Professional Master's Program Update</h2>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">By Kaitlyn Heinlein, Program Coordinator</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">One year down, many more to go! Our inaugural cohort finished strong this past August, with all of its students having successfully secured a job within the field. Now, in our second year of the program, we find ourselves growing with a new cohort of 13 students, which is about four times the size of last year's class. We had to name creative adjustments on the BH floor of AOS. What used to be a small, intimate program is now an office space for the Professional Master's students - a dedicated space where they can meet and mingle. Overall, a majority of this year's cohort are specializing in the Meteorological Forecasting and Modeling pathway, while a few tackle the Climate Science, Risk Management, and Communications pathway. In their search for summer</p>
            <h3 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-top: 30px;">CPEX-AW Field Campaign Reflection</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">By Ben Rodenkirch, Graduate Student</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">This past August, I had the privilege of participating in NASA's Convective Processes Experiment - Aerosols & Winds (CPEX-AW) field campaign based in St. Cloud, MN. For two weeks, I was tasked with collecting flight planning operations as both a lead and support aerostat. Additionally, I flew aboard NASA's DC-8 aircraft for the entire duration of the campaign. I was tasked with taking detailed logs of the atmospheric conditions and was responsible for deploying a few dropsonde! Finally, while I was there, I got to work on some aboard science flights, I assisted with, and helped to process some of the data collected multiple times for any given day.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">2022 internships, the students have expressed interest within the following areas: operational forecasting, weather/energy management, science communications, broadcast meteorology, and climate risk analysis. Please reach out to {{ coordinator_email }} if you have any questions or are interested in an internship. Otherwise, recruitment for Fall 2022 is underway. Admissions are open, and applications are already rolling in. If you know anyone who may be interested in the program, please send them to our website: {{ program_website }} and apply. We are proud of what the Professional Master's program has accomplished since its start in 2020 and excited to see what its future holds!</p>
            <h3 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-top: 30px;">Solstice Party 2022</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">The best social gathering hosted by the Student Chapter of the American Meteorological Society at the University of Wisconsin-Madison is scheduled for:</p>
            <p style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold; margin-top: 10px;">Mid-February 2022</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">Tickets will go on sale mid-January 2022. Watch our social media platforms and email. We hope you will join us!</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

