            <h2 style="font-size: 18pt;">What AOS has done for me:</h2>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">By Kris Craven, Meteorologist-in-Charge (MIC), National Weather Service Topeka</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">I have always loved things related to nature. From being fascinated by bugs, dinosaurs, and icebergs as a kid, to staring for hours at the maps on the walls of my grandfather's house, I was always drawn to things in the natural world. In the early days of my career, I was always drawn to things in the natural world. In the early days of my career, I was always drawn to things in the natural world. In the early days of my career, I was always drawn to things in the natural world.</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">I figured if I was going to watch radar data - at which point, I figured I was going to watch radar data all the time, I might as well</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">do it for a living. After some booster math and science courses at KU in Geophysics, I came up to UW-Madison for work as a Meteorologist and forecaster, and I have been lucky enough to have been able to work on the radar warning desk! The work that I did both as a teaching assistant and as a research assistant was invaluable. The research gave me considerable</p>
            <p style="font-size: 11pt;">insight into how weather modeling and numerical weather prediction work, and teaching and mentoring my organizational and people skills. I have been in the NWS for over 20 years, starting out as an intern in Kansas, and now I am a MIC in Topeka, Kansas. I have moved up to the Meteorologist in Charge position, and I have had a huge impact on my life and career.</p>
        </div>
